From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 08:00:36 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] venus: bound a host image copy by its footprint

vkCopyImageToMemoryMESA points the driver straight at the reply blob so the
temporary allocation and copy are skipped, and vkCopyMemoryToImageMESA
points it at the command data the guest sent.  Both are as long as the guest
chose, while the driver reaches as far as the copy region's footprint, so a
guest that sizes either too small lets the driver write or read past
renderer memory.

Compute that footprint as Vulkan addresses memory for buffer-image copies:
from the region, the image's format and array layer count, which the
renderer now records when the image is created, and the texel block of that
format and aspect as the Vulkan registry's format table gives it.  A format
or aspect the table cannot state exactly is sized at 32 bytes a texel, more
than any format holds, and a MEMCPY copy also counts the driver's memcpy
size for its layers.  A region whose footprint fits the guest's memory is
copied in place.  Otherwise the driver gets renderer memory of the whole
footprint, zeroed for an upload, and only the bytes the guest allocated or
sent move between it and the guest.  A region whose footprint cannot be
stated, because its layers lie outside the image or its size overflows, is
refused.

The region is also held to the image and to what the guest sent.  The
driver indexes the image's own tables by the region's mip level, aspect and
extent and checks none of them, so a region must name a mip level the image
has (its level count is recorded with its format), one aspect its format has,
and an extent that lies inside that level.  And the footprint may exceed
the guest's memory only by the 32 bytes a texel the table assumes for a
format it cannot state, since the guest sized its memory for the region it
copies: a row length or image height chosen freely cannot ask the renderer
for memory the guest never accounted for.

The same bounds hold for the other host image commands.  The readback stages
its footprint in memory the renderer allocated, and the driver writes the
texels of a padded row and leaves the padding between them, so the staging
starts zeroed: the reply must not carry what the allocator last held.  A
chroma plane (the second and third plane of a multi-planar format) is as
large as its subsampling leaves it, half the image across and down in a 4:2:0
format and half across in 4:2:2, rounded down and halved per mip level to one
texel as the driver lays the plane out, and a region is held to that: an image
whose extent the subsampling does not divide has fewer texels in the plane
than the rounded-up quotient, so the driver would reach past its texture and,
in an image-to-image copy, past its temporary.  And
vkCopyImageToImage, which the driver copies through one temporary the size of
the source plane, refuses a null image, a region outside either image, layers
that differ between the two sides, and a destination that takes more bytes
than the source gave, with VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY as the memory copies
do.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
